## Generics Meet Collections

When you combine generic functions with generic collections, you write algorithms that work for EVERY type.

### Generic Counter for Any Vec

```xiom
fn count_items[T](items: &Vec[T]) -> Int {
  return items.len();
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let nums: Vec[Int] = Vec[Int].new();
  nums.push(1); nums.push(2);

  let words: Vec[Str] = Vec[Str].new();
  words.push("hi");

  io.println(string.int_to_str(count_items(&nums)));
  io.println(string.int_to_str(count_items(&words)));
  return 0;
}
```

One `count_items` function works for `Vec[Int]`, `Vec[Str]`, `Vec[Bool]`, `Vec[Person]` — anything.

### Generic Find in Vec

```xiom
fn find_index[T](items: &Vec[T], target: &T) -> Option[Int] {
  let i = 0;
  while i < items.len() {
    let item = match items.get(i) { Some(v) => v, None => continue };
    // Comparing generic items requires equality support
    // For now, use index-based approach
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return None;
}
```

Wait — comparing generic items with `==` requires the items to support equality. In a fully generic function, you'd need an interface constraint (L6 topic!). For now, we can count, get by index, and push — operations that work on any T.

### Generic Operations That Always Work

```xiom
fn first[T](items: &Vec[T]) -> Option[T] {
  return items.get(0);
}

fn last[T](items: &Vec[T]) -> Option[T] {
  if items.len() == 0 {
    return None;
  }
  return items.get(items.len() - 1);
}

fn is_empty[T](items: &Vec[T]) -> Bool {
  return items.len() == 0;
}

fn push_pair[T](items: &mut Vec[T], a: T, b: T) {
  items.push(a);
  items.push(b);
}
```

These work because they don't depend on any property of T. `.len()`, `.get(i)`, `.push(v)` — these are Vec operations, not T operations.

